WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.240
CHRISTIAN TUEFFERS:
Welcome to the session

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.690
"Master Security and Compliance
in the Public Cloud."

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.430
My name is Christian
Tueffers, and I'm

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.910
the senior Cloud architect
with Deutsche Boerse AG.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.190
I'm doing the session
together with Grace Morrison

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:18.690
from Google Cloud.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.390
In the beginning, I would like
to give you a short overview

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.140
on Deutsche Boerse Group.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.300
But we are a so-called market
infrastructure provider,

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.060
and as such, technology
is really in our DNA.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.480
Our first fully-automated
electronic trading system

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.610
was launched in the late '90s.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.470
This one is operating
on the lowest latency

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:42.700
networks and technology, and
together with our core location

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.550
partners, we are operating
one of the largest European

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.170
financial data centers.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:50.240
All of our core
business applications

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.190
are actually self-developed
self-maintained,

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:57.360
and we do this with round
about 2000 people in our IT.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.450
Our core is really our trading
and clearing applications.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:04.790
As you can see, we use them not
only for securities trading,

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.790
but in the end, for any kind
of financial derivatives

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:10.580
trading, currency
trading, but also trading

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.570
of commodities like energy.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:15.000
In addition,
Deutsche Boerse Group

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.920
is offering
pre-trading services.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.120
So we calculate return
indexes like, for example,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.290
the [INAUDIBLE] and the stocks.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:25.320
We also operate
ultra-low latency data

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.900
feeds for our customers.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.390
The post-trading area is dealing
with settlement and custody

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:35.140
services, and in addition, we
offer value-added services,

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.640
like investment fund services
or collateral management.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:44.420
Everything of this is backed
by our information technology.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.750
As you can see, we are
really a core element

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.570
with the European and
global capital market,

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:52.090
and as such, we are under
scrutiny of the highest

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.280
regulatory supervision--

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.560
not only by the
German regulator,

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.680
but also by regulations in
Luxembourg, Switzerland,

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.460
and the UK.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.040
Deutsche Boerse Group
is using public cloud

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:08.350
as one of the main technologies
to drive our roadmap 2020.

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.840
Public cloud is
fueling our foundations

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.170
for other technologies
like, for example,

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.160
blockchain-based services.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.170
We develop and run
blockchain-based services

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:20.370
for digital assets
or digital custody.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.860
In addition, we
leverage public cloud

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:29.430
for analyzing big data sets
for of us or of our customers.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.430
And we also make use of
machine learning capabilities.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:36.400
So you can see, public cloud
is adding a lot of agility

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.910
into our mix, because with
this, we can react dynamically

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:42.010
to changing business
demands and we

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.680
can leverage new technologies
to develop new solutions

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.100
for our customers.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.400
But we use public
cloud also to improve

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.980
the overall quality
of our systems,

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.800
and this is achieved by
standardization and automation,

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.990
and [INAUDIBLE] the
additional possibilities

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:59.270
which public cloud
offered to us.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:03.170
As a good example, our core
trading platform, called G7,

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:05.180
is spinning up
every night hundreds

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.940
of test instances
in order to conduct

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:09.660
some kind of random tests--

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.420
so-called "monkey tests."

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:14.220
And then, based upon
the behavior of this,

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.310
we can improve the
overall quality.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.620
Of course, we use
public cloud also

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.680
to reduce the operational
cost by standardization

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.510
and automation.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:28.180
We are implementing a so-called
multi-cloud hybrid cloud

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:29.910
strategy.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.130
As you can see on the
lower right corner,

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:35.220
we are not leveraging only
the Google Cloud platform.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.663
In addition, we team up
with all the hyperscalers

00:03:38.663 --> 00:03:40.080
in the infrastructure
as a service

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.210
and platform as a service area.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.550
And also, for
software as a service,

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:48.620
we work together with
the market leaders.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.810
The multi-cloud strategy
is important to us

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:53.630
because it allows us to
select really the best

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:56.150
service for each use case.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.990
But in addition, it also
prevents the vendor lock-in.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:03.570
And this is something which
is demanded by the regulators.

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:07.440
So whenever we move workloads
into the public cloud,

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.470
we have to provide a
so-called exit strategy.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.710
The exit strategy could be
that we either fall back

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.680
to on-premise, or that
we move the workload

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.820
from one public cloud into
the other public cloud.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:24.140
In order to do so, we
implemented a hybrid approach,

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.170
leveraging technologies like
container and Kubernetes.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:30.260
This allows us to
dynamically shift workloads

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.870
between on-premise and to
different cloud service

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:34.740
providers.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:38.580
Now we know, of course,
that this is not just

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:41.430
on a quick switch,
but in the end,

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.890
it's sufficient
that we can prove

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:45.540
that within a
certain timeframe, we

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.000
can move workloads
out of one cloud

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.990
and move it back to another one.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:54.460
Now let's look into
the security aspects.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.680
This picture, I guess, is
very familiar to most of you.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.140
It outlines the so-called
Shared Responsibility Model.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.940
On the lower part, you
see the responsibilities

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:05.440
of the cloud service
provider, which

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.540
deal with the so-called
security of the cloud.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:11.560
This covers, typically, the
data centers, physical hardware

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.410
network up to the hypervisor
level, but also the cloud

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:17.680
services itself.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.220
And also, the IT
processes in the back--

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.690
things like access management,
incident management,

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:26.260
or capacity management.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.400
On top of that, you have
the security in the cloud.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.100
This is our responsibility
as a customer.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.890
So we have to make
sure that we provide

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:35.080
secure access into the cloud.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.720
We have to define
appropriate roles.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:41.200
We have to define security
guard [? trades ?] to make sure

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.590
that we don't compromise on
the high level of security

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:48.000
when we move into
the public cloud.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.460
Typically, the lower
part is tackled

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.460
by looking at external
certifications,

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.970
external reports,
like all ISO 27000,

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:59.460
SOC [? 2.2 ?]
reports, and so on.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:02.220
But the regulator made
it crystal clear to us

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:04.500
that only looking at these
kind of certifications

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.160
and attestations by the
better party providers

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:09.220
is not sufficient.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:11.380
They deem the use
of cloud services

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:13.920
similar like the
classical outsourcing,

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.760
and hence, we need to
have the same control

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.620
mechanisms in place.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:20.560
And one of them is that we need
to have an unrestricted right

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.560
to audit, that we really
can uncover and look

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:25.840
into the details how
the cloud service

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:31.110
provider is implementing all
the required security controls.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:34.450
Now, if you would do this alone,
this would mean a lot of effort

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:37.558
for us, because as you
have seen previously,

00:06:37.558 --> 00:06:39.850
we work with a lot of different
cloud service providers

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:41.230
together.

00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:45.050
And an audit is
typically an engagement

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:48.050
which takes a couple of
weeks or even months,

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:51.408
and you'll need a lot of
people in order to do so.

00:06:51.408 --> 00:06:52.950
On the other side,
there's also a lot

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.658
of effort for the
cloud service provider,

00:06:54.658 --> 00:06:57.570
because it's not only us
having this unrestricted right

00:06:57.570 --> 00:07:02.500
to audit, but in the end, all
institutes in the same area.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:05.970
So for example in all the
financial service institutes.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:07.480
Hence, we came up
a couple of years

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:10.810
ago with the idea of a
collaborative audit group.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.000
The collaborative audit
group is an open format,

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.400
where financial service
institutes jointly

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:19.560
together conduct an audit with
the cloud service provider.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:23.450
This has been proven
in 2017 the first time,

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:26.060
in a small proof of concept.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:30.905
Then 2018 was really the first
large-scale audit conducted.

00:07:30.905 --> 00:07:34.280
And in 2019 also,
Google Cloud platform

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:37.940
was audited by the
collaborative audit group.

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:41.060
Currently, there is round about
40 different financial service

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:44.960
institutes in the collaborative
audit group, but as mentioned,

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:49.060
any financial institute
can join herein.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:51.190
For the security
in the cloud part,

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:55.640
we based ourself on the
ISO 27001 framework.

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:58.880
Together with our information
security colleagues,

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.880
we identified seven
focus domains,

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:05.510
where we put a lot of effort
into implementing security

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:08.600
controls to achieve the
high level of security which

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:13.520
we are known for our on-premise
infrastructure, as well.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.270
So the focus domains are here
shaded in blue, and cover

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:19.410
things like asset management,
access control, cryptography,

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.810
and so on.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:23.750
So we concluded
with our colleagues

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:25.940
that we needed 20 different
initiatives in order

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:29.800
to implement all the required
controls to cover dimension

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:31.490
as a domains.

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:33.710
So as you can see,
this is pretty much

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:36.825
a mix of where we extend
over the existing solutions.

00:08:36.825 --> 00:08:38.450
A good example is
the cyber [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:40.720
for privileged
access management,

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:44.350
or where we identified
the cloud native services

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:47.330
as appropriate-- for example,
in the area of key management

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:49.750
and sequence management.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:52.620
Sometimes, also we
identified that there

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:54.910
is neither a solution
on-premise available

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:57.010
nor a cloud [INAUDIBLE]
solution available,

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:58.810
and we did an evaluation
of a new tool.

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:02.140
A good example here is AquaSec
for compliance checking

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:04.090
of containers in our
Kubernetes setup.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:11.700
Now, as you can see, applying
security in the cloud

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:12.895
is a lot of effort.

00:09:12.895 --> 00:09:15.565
It's a lot of effort
because, on one side,

00:09:15.565 --> 00:09:17.980
there's always new
requirements coming

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:21.890
from the regulators and new
security threats out there.

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:24.970
And on the other side, also
the cloud service providers

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:29.200
are always offering new
services and new possibilities.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:31.260
But what we found
is that in the end,

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:34.350
applying security in the
cloud is something which

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:37.180
is the same for every client.

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:39.600
So it's not only us
doing the configuration,

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:42.720
doing the security guard
[INAUDIBLE] configuration,

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.740
the role definition, and so on.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:47.460
And yet, every client, at least
under the same regulation,

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:49.990
has to redo the same effort.

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:53.200
And we thought, how could
this be made more efficient?

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:54.940
And the idea we
discussed with Google

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:58.750
here is that the cloud service
provider could take back

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:02.770
some part of the responsibility
of the security in the cloud

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:05.950
by providing things like
certified landing zones, which

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:08.120
are compliant by default.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:11.920
And I think this not only
increases the security posture

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:14.830
for the clients and puts
down the barriers of moving

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:17.890
really critical
workloads into the cloud,

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:20.710
we think this is also
increasing the overall security

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:22.940
culture for Google.

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:26.500
And this is why we came into
close discussion with Grace.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:30.200
And thankfully now, I'll hand
over for her to continue.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:32.220
GRACE MOLLISON:
Thank you, Christian.

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:36.060
So Christian called
out some focus domains

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:40.770
from the 14 controlled
domains in ISO 27001.

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:44.280
Google Cloud is able to
provide guardrails and controls

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:47.610
to address these with native
tooling, as well as being

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:49.860
able to integrate
partner solutions to help

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:52.240
with implementing the controls.

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:53.970
I'm just showing
here some native ways

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:56.790
to help meet the requirements,
but we have a rich partner

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:59.670
ecosystem that can also help so
you can leverage your existing

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:03.163
investment in those
partner solutions.

00:11:03.163 --> 00:11:04.580
Today, however,
I'm going to focus

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:07.740
on responding to these
challenges Christian set up.

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:10.170
How do we address
security in the cloud

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:12.900
for many customers, who all
have different business goals

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:14.250
and applications?

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:16.470
How do we make it
efficient to address?

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:18.480
How do we clearly
distinguish who does what

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:20.370
in a shared
responsibility model,

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:23.800
and what about
certified landing zones?

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:25.380
So Christian is right.

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:27.310
It's the same challenge
for every customer,

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:30.210
and taking a cross-sample of
the questions we've been asked,

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:32.820
you can see that other customers
are asking the same things

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:33.920
Christian has asked--

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:36.060
can I run my regulated workload?

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:38.560
What products are certified
for the regulational standard

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:39.990
I need to comply with?

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:43.866
What do I need to do to
achieve my security posture?

00:11:43.866 --> 00:11:46.550
Our customers then say that
we also want a single place

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:50.340
to find the assets we need to
address our security posture.

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:52.860
We responded with the Google
Cloud Compliance Resource

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:54.090
Center.

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:55.860
Security Health Analytics
is an exception,

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:58.242
but I'll come back to that.

00:11:58.242 --> 00:11:59.950
The Compliance Resource
Center is the way

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:02.630
to find Google Cloud's
compliance offerings by region

00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:05.890
and industry, with best
practices and documentation

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:09.060
to support your compliance
reporting needs.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:12.060
You can gain access to Google
Cloud's attestations reports

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.370
to confirm compliance with
a variety of standards

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:16.950
and regulations.

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:18.960
Google Cloud provides
a variety of security

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.240
guidance to help customers
deploy and develop

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.370
with a security-first mindset.

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:26.730
Customers can satisfy themselves
that they can meet the security

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:29.670
requirements by Google
Cloud providing guidance

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:31.920
and the ability for
customers to implement

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.310
the guardrails required.

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:37.450
We want to provide prescriptive
guidance and easily-deployable

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:41.410
assets which collectively
are security blueprints.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.110
Christian, you may
recall, said what

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:46.390
the idea of a blueprint where
the CSP, i.e., the Cloud

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:49.190
Service Provider, takes
part of responsibility

00:12:49.190 --> 00:12:52.010
within the cloud, and also
provides certified landing

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:56.290
zones that are compliant
by default. So we do

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:58.670
have security blueprints.

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:00.970
Google Cloud's definition
of the blueprint

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:04.250
provides a package of
documentation and assets,

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:07.720
which can be consumed all stages
of the customer lifecycle.

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:09.450
The intended users
are folks working

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:12.640
in the customer's compliance and
governance function, and also

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:14.620
security administrators.

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:18.100
They may be of interest to other
users, business or technical,

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:21.680
to determine, for example, if
certain use cases are feasible.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:23.860
A blueprint package
varies in size,

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:27.500
based on complexity
of the use case.

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:30.480
So Google Cloud has two types
of security blueprints--

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:34.530
compliant landing zones--
for example, GKE for PCI--

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:36.780
and product and workload
focus, such as those

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:38.940
to Anthos, or for
your [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:40.700
the warehouse pipeline.

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:43.660
The compliant landing zone
blueprints have more components

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:45.720
than the workload
focus blueprints,

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.300
but both types have the
core components that

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:51.510
are the what, which explains
what controls you need

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:53.250
and what you need
to do, and the how,

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:56.950
which are the deployable
assets and instructions.

00:13:56.950 --> 00:13:59.540
The first compliant landing
zone blueprint published

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:00.920
is PCI for GKE.

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:03.670
GKE is a Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:14:03.670 --> 00:14:06.850
This has a number of
deployable assets,

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:09.130
available in a
Github repo, provides

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:12.330
comprehensive guidance, includes
things like a quick start,

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:17.770
pre-rec so you can configure
your workstation, and diagrams,

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:22.075
and lots of them help known
issues and limitations

00:14:22.075 --> 00:14:22.825
and helpful links.

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:26.700
Our first product-specific
blueprint collection

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:28.180
is for Anthos.

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:29.440
The first three are published.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:31.478
And we are working on more.

00:14:31.478 --> 00:14:33.520
Let's take a closer look
at one of the blueprints

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:36.620
in the collection,
Locality-Restricted Workloads,

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:40.930
but first up, I'll give you
a short overview of Anthos.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:44.340
Anthos is an open and hybrid
cloud application platform,

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:47.580
which basically is managed
Kubernetes for GKE.

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:51.630
It allows you to run GKE where
you need to-- on Google Cloud,

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:53.580
on Premises, or another cloud.

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:56.940
It provides a consistent way
to manage your GKE clusters

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.450
in a secure manner.

00:14:59.450 --> 00:15:01.640
Anthos has a
security-first approach.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:04.940
It provides an auditable
and centralized workflow

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:08.480
to deploy conflict changes,
which both your security admins

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:10.160
and operations team [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:12.680
It allows you to
enforce compliance.

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:16.130
Anthos service mesh, which
is a key part of the Anthos

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:18.140
platform, abstracts
some of the lower-level

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:20.390
security actions
needed to secure

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:25.420
your microservices with MTLS
and certificate management.

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:26.920
Now, this isn't a
session on Anthos,

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:28.730
so don't worry if you don't
know what all these things I'm

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:29.900
showing you here are.

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:32.510
The key thing is that to be
able to configure your Anthos

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:34.790
environment to meet your
locality restriction

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:38.480
requirement, to enforce where
your applications are deployed

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:40.430
or where they can
be accessed from,

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:42.180
you need to consider
a number of things,

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:44.390
such as where your
services are located,

00:15:44.390 --> 00:15:46.610
and you need to configure
a number of other things

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:50.020
to address these considerations.

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:52.010
So making a simple
prescriptive to configure

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:55.260
is the aim of the
security blueprint.

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:57.390
To provide prescriptive
guidance to help

00:15:57.390 --> 00:16:00.090
our customers secure their
applications takes a village.

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:02.100
It starts with our
customers, you know,

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:05.430
so we work with our engineering
teams, UX designers, and tech

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:07.560
editors to show we're
delivering guides

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:10.680
that are not too jargon-heavy,
but validated and easy

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:11.700
to consume.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:13.870
In effect, we partner
with our customers

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:16.770
so the guidance we are providing
is not creating a bubble,

00:16:16.770 --> 00:16:20.100
and has customer input from
the beginning of the process.

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:23.170
But we didn't actually
stop at blueprints.

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:26.250
Our customers want to know
what Google Cloud products are

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:29.423
compliant with a given standard,
the shared responsibility

00:16:29.423 --> 00:16:31.590
model, and what steps they
need to take to configure

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:33.510
those products so they comply.

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:37.230
So the Security Command
Center is the answer to that.

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:41.140
It's the canonical security and
risk database for Google Cloud.

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:45.330
It is an intuitive, intelligent
risk dashboard and analytics

00:16:45.330 --> 00:16:48.620
system for servicing,
understanding, and remediating

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:52.980
Google Cloud Security and data
risks across an organization.

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:56.730
Note, though, it's
not a substitute

00:16:56.730 --> 00:17:00.150
for audit certification
or reports

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:02.100
of compliance of your
products or services,

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:05.130
with any regulatory or industry
benchmarks or standards.

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:07.060
It's a guide to help you.

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:09.270
The key feature the
Security Command Center

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:11.190
is Security Health Analytics.

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:13.200
In the Premium tier,
it provides monitoring

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:15.450
for many industry-best
practices,

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:18.720
and compliance monitoring
across your Google Cloud assets.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:20.339
These results can
also be reviewed

00:17:20.339 --> 00:17:23.060
in a compliance dashboard.

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.400
So here's an example of the
Security Health Analytics

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:26.319
dashboard.

00:17:26.319 --> 00:17:27.980
We're using in the Premium tier.

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:29.930
It's a single place to
view how well you're

00:17:29.930 --> 00:17:31.888
mapping against requirements.

00:17:31.888 --> 00:17:33.680
Now, this is a mouthful,
sorry about this--

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:37.850
the CIS Google Cloud Foundation
1.0 mappings have been reviewed

00:17:37.850 --> 00:17:41.720
and certified by the Center for
Internet Security for Alignment

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:44.180
for the CIS Google
Cloud Computing

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:49.070
Foundation's benchmarks B1.0.0.

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:52.620
Additional compliance mappings
are included for reference,

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:56.330
and are not provided or reviewed
by PCI or the [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:56.330 --> 00:17:57.510
Foundation.

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:59.650
And I just want to
reiterate, as I said earlier,

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:02.770
it's not a substitute for
audit certification or report

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:04.520
of compliance of your
products or services

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:08.333
with any regulatory or industry
benchmarks or standards.

00:18:08.333 --> 00:18:10.250
Still looking at the
Security Health Analytics

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:12.540
from the Findings page, you
can click on the category,

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:14.248
and we'll provide
guidance as to what you

00:18:14.248 --> 00:18:15.980
need to do to remediate, OK?

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:19.032
So it's a powerful
tool to help you.

00:18:19.032 --> 00:18:20.990
So I just want to kind
of bring it all together

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:24.950
and talk about the user journey
to use these tools that I've

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:26.150
talked about.

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:28.580
You've just heard what Google
Cloud has done in response

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:30.890
to our customers' request
to make it easier,

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:33.410
by providing the Compliance
Resource Center as the place

00:18:33.410 --> 00:18:34.520
to go.

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:37.190
Customers have many
personas involved

00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:39.680
in ensuring that they meet
the compliance security

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:41.900
requirements, so I
just want to summarize

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:44.175
by walking through a
typical customer's journey

00:18:44.175 --> 00:18:45.800
through the Compliance
Resource Center,

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:48.360
showing how the various
personas interact with it.

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:51.620
So it normally starts with
the CISO, in many cases, who

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:54.680
wants to check that Google Cloud
can meet the desired compliance

00:18:54.680 --> 00:18:57.920
regime, and maybe
validation at this stage

00:18:57.920 --> 00:18:59.660
what products are in scope.

00:18:59.660 --> 00:19:03.243
If that gets the OK, then the
security admins and architects

00:19:03.243 --> 00:19:04.910
start looking at what
Google Cloud could

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:07.910
be used for, and the
configuration they need

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:10.820
to be in, by using the
compliance map, Security Health

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:13.360
Analytics, and the blueprints.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:15.770
And then the platform
operations security

00:19:15.770 --> 00:19:19.130
admins and devs, knowing
what security postures need

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:21.230
to be met, can use the
Security Health Analytics

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:23.035
and blueprints to
achieve that when

00:19:23.035 --> 00:19:24.410
they configure
their environments

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:26.333
and deploy their applications.

00:19:26.333 --> 00:19:28.250
So everyone in the journey
to meet [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:33.240
compliance and security posture
are using the same assets.

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:38.230
So thank you for listening, and
I hope that was of some use.

